 Goal

  - High-level objective
      - Build from source and modify PostgreSQL and pg_duckdb to enable online ML-based routing: for each incoming query,
  predict whether PostgreSQL or DuckDB will be faster and route accordingly, then evaluate end-to-end performance versus single-
  engine baselines.
  - Hard constraints
      - Do not use Docker inside the container.
      - Use the HTTP proxy http://127.0.0.1:7890 for all network downloads.
      - Do not use system-wide PostgreSQL or any system services; install under /workspace only.
      - Collect real, end-to-end latency measurements (no simulation or fabricated data).
  - Environment setup
      - Export proxy env vars for every fetch/build tool:
      - export http_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:7890
      - export https_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:7890
      - export HTTP_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:7890
      - export HTTPS_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:7890
  - Directory layout under /workspace:
      - src/postgres, src/duckdb, src/pg_duckdb
      - build/local (install prefix), build/artifacts, experiments/<session>/results
      - Ensure PATH/LD_LIBRARY_PATH pick local builds only.
  - Source code retrieval
      - Git clone with proxy:
      - PostgreSQL: https://github.com/postgres/postgres.git (use a stable tag, e.g., REL_16_STABLE or v16.x)
      - DuckDB: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb.git (stable tag)
      - pg_duckdb: https://github.com/duckdb/pg_duckdb.git (compatible with chosen PG version)
  - Build from source (no system services)
      - PostgreSQL:
      - ./configure --prefix=/workspace/build/local/pg --without-readline --without-zlib
      - make -j && make install
      - Use the built pg_config from /workspace/build/local/pg/bin/pg_config for all PG extensions.
  - DuckDB:
      - Build CLI and library (CMake or make) into /workspace/build/local/duckdb
      - Ensure a CLI or library is available to run queries locally (no Docker).
  - pg_duckdb:
      - export PG_CONFIG=/workspace/build/local/pg/bin/pg_config
      - make && make install (installs into local PG extension dir)
  - Data and schema
      - Create a small benchmark schema both in PostgreSQL (using the built binaries) and in DuckDB (using the built CLI):
      - Tables: customers(1000), products(200), sales(~50k rows) with indexes relevant for joins/filters.
      - Prepare a workload of 24–36 SQL queries covering filters, joins, group-by, aggregates, order-by with varied selectivity.
  - Feature extraction (pre-optimization features)
      - In PostgreSQL source or via a small PG extension “pg_mlrouter”:
      - Extract pre-optimization features available in/near parse/analyze phases or early planner hook:
        - counts: tables, joins, join types, predicates by operator class, aggregates, group-by cols
  - Dual-execution data collection
      - For each query:
  shutdown after runs.
  - Model training (offline)
        - `int predict_engine(const features_t* f); // 0=postgres, 1=duckdb`
      - Keep the model small enough to compile quickly (depth-limited tree or few trees).
  - Online integration (PostgreSQL)
      - Create a Postgres extension “pg_mlrouter” (in src/pg_mlrouter) that:
      - Hooks at a planner_hook or ExecutorStart (whichever is simpler).
      - Builds features_t from the incoming query (using the feature helper).
      - Calls predict_engine to decide route:
        - If predict_engine==1 (DuckDB faster): route query via pg_duckdb (pushdown/FDW or function call).
        - Else: run normally in PostgreSQL.
      - Provide a GUC to disable/enable routing for A/B testing.
  - Build/install the extension with PG_CONFIG pointing to local pg build. Ensure shared_preload_libraries or suitable hook
  registration without touching system configuration.
  - End-to-end experiments and evaluation
      - Run the workload again in three modes:
      - PostgreSQL-only
      - DuckDB-only
      - ML-router (pg_mlrouter enabled)
  - For each mode, run each query 5× and record medians; compute:
      - Router accuracy against ground-truth winner
      - End-to-end latency and geometric mean speedup vs. the best single-engine baseline
      - Overhead from feature extraction and routing
  - Save artifacts:
      - results/dataset.csv, results/model.json, src/pg_mlrouter/ml_router.[ch], results/summary.json
  - Final deliverables
      - Write experiments//results/final.json with:
      - success: true/false
      - data: { dataset_path, model_path, router_src_paths, metrics: {accuracy, speedup, overhead}, per-query results }
      - analysis: description of approach, limitations, future work
      - conclusions: bullet-list of findings
      - errors: if any
  - Robustness and fallbacks
      - If some build steps fail, provide clear error logs and partial results. Do not use system PostgreSQL or Docker. Keep
  everything under /workspace. Always try to produce final.json with success=false and an errors list if full success isn’t
  possible.